<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- The gear on the start page, in two steps of the surface ramp: raised at the top left falling to
     card at the bottom right. Angle 315 because Android measures anticlockwise from east, so that is
     the top-left corner; it must be a multiple of 45.

     The ramp climbs away from the ground in both appearances, and away is lighter in the dark one and
     darker in the light one - so this reads as lit from the top left on a dark ground and as shaded
     from it on a light one. Left as one drawable on purpose: correcting the direction needs a second
     one chosen by Prefs.isLight, which is a theme overlay's worth of machinery for a 1.1 step across
     48dp seen from three metres.

     It is a drawable and not a backgroundTint because a tint is one colour: a MaterialButton's
     background is its shape drawable and tinting it cannot hold two. So this sits behind the button in
     its own box and the button keeps its shape, its ripple and its focus contour, with a transparent
     tint of its own. A gradient in a <shape> works from API 21; only a gradient inside a
     VectorDrawable would need 24, which is why the subtitle preview is built the same way. -->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="oval">
    <gradient
        android:angle="315"
        android:endColor="?attr/accentCard"
        android:startColor="?attr/accentRaised"
        android:type="linear" />
</shape>
